---

`save` (lib/IO/K8s/Role/APIObject.pm:685-691) does `open my $fh, '>', $file` and prints `to_yaml`, which is a character string (YAML::PP `dump_string`; t/05_utf8.t feeds it straight back into `load_yaml`, which wants characters). Without `:encoding(UTF-8)`:

- a document whose non-ASCII characters are all below U+0100 (German, French, Spanish text in an annotation or ConfigMap) is written as Latin-1 - not valid UTF-8. `load_yaml` on that file does not return what was saved, and kubectl reads garbage;
- any character above U+00FF makes Perl emit UTF-8 with a 'Wide character in print' warning.

```
perl -Ilib -MIO::K8s -e 'my $k=IO::K8s->new; my $f="/tmp/s.yaml"; $k->new_object("ConfigMap",{metadata=>{name=>"x"},data=>{a=>"gr\x{fc}n"}})->save($f); print $k->load_yaml($f)->[0]->data->{a} eq "gr\x{fc}n" ? "same" : "DIFFERENT", "\n"'
# DIFFERENT      (file holds 67 72 fc 6e)
```

k159 fixed the reading side (`_slurp_utf8`); the writing side was left. The POD of `to_yaml` in Role::Resource also calls the result 'a YAML byte string' (lib/IO/K8s/Role/Resource.pm:578), which it is not.

Done when: `save` writes UTF-8 (`'>:encoding(UTF-8)'`, close checked), a save/load_yaml round trip with Latin-1-range and wide characters is tested, and the POD says characters for to_yaml and UTF-8 for save.

